In article <1993Apr28.121214.19383@osf.org> rsalz@osf.org (Rich Salz) writes:

   In <C6601o.n2E@da_vinci.it.uswc.uswest.com> Jeffrey L. DeMent <jld@advtech.uswest.com> writes:
   >I am trying to validate a "rumor" that DCE Version 1.0.2 contains
   >approximately 1700 bug fixes over v1.0.1 -- this sounds too high.

   This is in the right ballpark.  Note that it includes documentation changes.
   About one-half to one-third are in DFS.

I'll also note that: *every* *single* *change* to the DCE1.0.2 source
tree required that a defect report be opened to track the change.
I.e., if you wanted to (e.g.,) correct a misspelling in an error
message, you had to open a defect report to cover it.

Each additional quantum of functionality added requrired a new defect
report, too (though the 1700 may not have included the
"enhancements").

Transient things along the lines of compile errors which were
introduced on day N and fixed on day N+1 were also often tracked under
new defect reports and were probably counted in the above totals.

(I'll also add my opinion that "n defects per KLOC" is a bogus metric.
It's really "n *known* defects per KLOC"; as a metric, it's also only
valid in combination with a "n% branch coverage" or "n% API coverage"
metric).

					- Bill














